export const K1={title:'CERT, the event and team organization',hook:'After a major earthquake or tornado, 911 lines jam, roads are blocked and professional responders may not reach a neighborhood for hours or days. Community Emergency Response Teams train ordinary people to help themselves, their families and their neighbors until help arrives. CERT Skills is a two-person team event that tests exactly those skills.',objectives:['Describe the CERT Skills event, its test plan and its five skills.','Explain the history and purpose of CERT and its place in emergency management.','Describe CERT organization, the Incident Command System and team roles.','Apply CERT priorities: rescuer safety and doing the greatest good for the greatest number.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'CERT Skills is a **team event for two competitors**. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes** (the team’s average counts): **life-threatening interventions and SALT triage 30%**, **medical operations 20%**, **disaster preparedness 15%**, **light search and rescue 15%**, and **CERT organization**, **disaster psychology**, **fire safety** and **terrorism 5% each**. References are FEMA’s **IS-317** online course, the **CERT Basic Training Participant Manual**, the Red Cross **First Aid for Severe Trauma (FAST)** course and the **SALT Mass Casualty Triage** program.',
'**Round Two** skills: **I** SALT triage (4 minutes, 2–3 patients), **II-A and II-B** treating life-threatening conditions—opening the airway (Rescuer 1) and controlling bleeding (Rescuer 2) (5 minutes), **III** head-to-toe assessment (5), **IV** lifts and carries (8) and **V** splinting a closed fracture (5). Teams bring a **disaster kit**: PPE (10 pairs of non-latex gloves each, eye protection, 2 masks, helmet and work gloves, sturdy shoes), bandaging materials, a tourniquet (or use HOSA’s), bandage scissors, blank patient tags and triage tape, paper, pens and alcohol-based hand rub. Teams need **70%** on the skills; ties go to the higher average test score.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Life-threatening interventions and SALT triage','30%'],['Medical operations','20%'],['Disaster preparedness','15%'],['Light search and rescue','15%'],['CERT organization','5%'],['Disaster psychology','5%'],['Fire safety','5%'],['Terrorism','5%']]}]],
['What CERT is',[
'The CERT concept was developed by the **Los Angeles Fire Department in 1985**, and its value was clear after the 1987 Whittier Narrows earthquake. **FEMA** made CERT training available nationwide in 1993. Thousands of CERT programs now exist in cities, counties, schools, campuses and workplaces. CERT members learn disaster preparedness, fire safety and utility control, disaster medical operations, light search and rescue, team organization, disaster psychology and terrorism awareness.',
'CERT volunteers **support**—never replace—professional responders. Their roles include preparing their own households, checking on neighbors, putting out small fires, treating injuries, conducting light search and rescue, helping with shelters and spreading preparedness messages. CERT members also assist with non-emergency community events and drills.',
sort('CERT role or not?',['Appropriate CERT role','Not a CERT role'],[['Shutting off natural gas when a leak is suspected',0],['Entering a collapsed building',1],['Treating bleeding while EMS is delayed',0],['Fighting a fully involved house fire',1],['Checking on elderly neighbors after a storm',0],['Directing traffic on a highway without authorization',1]])]],
['CERT priorities and rescuer safety',[
'The first CERT priority is **rescuer safety**: CERT members do not become victims themselves. Always wear PPE—**helmet, goggles or safety glasses, work gloves (over non-latex gloves for medical care), a dust mask and sturdy shoes or boots**—and work in **buddy teams**. Every skill begins with a **size-up**: gather facts, assess damage, consider probabilities, assess your own situation, set priorities, make decisions, develop a plan, take action and evaluate progress.',
'CERT’s goal is to **do the greatest good for the greatest number** of people in the shortest time with available resources. That is why triage—sorting victims by need—comes before detailed treatment, and why CERT members avoid unsafe rescues. Volunteers in most states are protected by **Good Samaritan laws** when they act in good faith within their training. **Consent**: conscious adults must agree to care; an **unconscious** person is assumed to give **implied consent**.',
order('CERT size-up',['Gather facts','Assess and communicate the damage','Consider probabilities','Assess your own situation','Establish priorities','Make decisions','Develop a plan of action','Take action','Evaluate progress'])]],
['Team organization and ICS',[
'CERTs use the **Incident Command System (ICS)**, the standard structure used by professional responders. The **team leader or incident commander** sets objectives; **sections** include **operations** (fire suppression, search and rescue, medical), **planning** (documentation and situation status), **logistics** (supplies, equipment and people) and **administration/finance**. **Span of control**—the number of people one leader supervises—should be about **3 to 7**, ideally **5**.',
'Teams establish a **command post**, **staging area** for resources, **treatment areas** and communication methods. Documentation—who is where, what was found and what was done—is critical. When professional responders arrive, CERT leaders give a briefing and follow their direction.',
match('ICS terms',[['Incident commander','Sets objectives and leads the response'],['Operations section','Search, rescue, fire and medical teams'],['Logistics section','Supplies, equipment and people'],['Planning section','Documentation and situation status'],['Span of control','3–7 people per supervisor, ideally 5'],['Staging area','Where resources wait for assignment']])]],
['Communication and documentation',[
'Teams communicate by radio, phone, runners or written messages. Messages should be **clear, concise and complete**: who, what, where, when and what is needed. CERT members document victims treated, locations searched, hazards found and resources used. Rating sheets reward **verbal and nonverbal communication** with patients, partners and the judge—so partners should talk to each other throughout each skill.',
kase('Who can be treated?',{a:{text:'You find an unconscious adult after a building partially collapses. Do you need permission to treat? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['No—an unconscious patient is assumed to give implied consent','b','Correct. Implied consent applies.',true],['Yes—find a family member first','b','Do not delay care for an unconscious patient.',false]]},b:{text:'A conscious adult refuses your help. What do you do?',choices:[['Respect the refusal, document it and report it','c','Correct. Conscious adults must consent.',true],['Treat them anyway','c','Treating without consent is not allowed.',false]]},c:{text:'You document both patients and report to your team leader.',end:true}})]],
["Working as a two-person team in Round Two",["CERT Skills is scored as a team, and several rating sheets give separate points to **Rescuer 1** and **Rescuer 2** for communication. Before the scenario starts, partners can don safety equipment and agree on roles—for example, Rescuer 1 leads airway and triage calls while Rescuer 2 handles bleeding control and documentation. Read the scenario together, decide whether to split (when the scenario allows) and talk throughout: “I’ll take Patient A; you take B.”","Practice every skill with a timer, switching roles so both partners can perform each skill. Verbalize steps that cannot be performed (helmet, boots, chair lift), use the patient’s words and keep your voice calm. A neat, labeled disaster kit saves time—keep gloves, tourniquet, gauze, triage tape and tags where both partners can reach them.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Team routine at the start of a skill","steps":["Don PPE (verbalize helmet, boots and gloves if needed)","Read the scenario together","Assign roles","Size up the scene aloud","Begin the skill and keep talking","Report findings to the judge"]}]]
],takeaways:['Team of two; test areas led by life-threatening interventions and SALT triage (30%); Round Two: SALT triage, airway and bleeding control, head-to-toe, lifts and carries, splinting; bring a full disaster kit.','CERT began with the LAFD in 1985 and became national through FEMA in 1993; volunteers support professionals; rescuer safety first; greatest good for the greatest number.','Use the size-up, PPE and buddy teams; ICS with an incident commander, operations, planning, logistics and admin; span of control 3–7; unconscious patients give implied consent.'],terms:[['CERT','Community Emergency Response Team—trained volunteers who help until professionals arrive.'],['Incident Command System','A standard organization for managing emergencies.'],['Implied consent','Assumed permission to treat an unconscious person.']],mcqs:[q('What kind of permission does a CERT volunteer need to treat an unconscious patient?','An unconscious patient is assumed to have given implied consent','Contact the nearest family member','An EMT must give permission','Contact the patient’s physician','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which percentage of the CERT test covers life-threatening interventions and SALT triage?','30%','5%','15%','50%','The largest area.'),q('Who developed the CERT concept?','The Los Angeles Fire Department','The FBI','The Red Cross only','The Department of Education','In 1985.'),q('What is CERT’s first priority?','Rescuer safety','Speed','Property','Paperwork','Do not become a victim.'),q('The ideal span of control is about:','5','15','1','25','Range 3–7.'),q('Which ICS section handles supplies and equipment?','Logistics','Operations','Planning','Command','Gets resources.'),q('The CERT goal is to do the greatest good for:','The greatest number of people','The first person found','Property owners','Pets only','Triage logic.'),q('Which PPE is required in CERT skills?','Helmet, eye protection, work gloves, mask and sturdy shoes','Only a mask','Only gloves','No PPE','Some may be verbalized.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the purpose of CERT, its priorities and how a CERT team is organized using the Incident Command System after a neighborhood earthquake.',rubric:[['Purpose',['support','professional responders','neighbors','until help arrives','fema','volunteers']],['Priorities',['rescuer safety','ppe','buddy','greatest good','greatest number','size-up']],['ICS structure',['incident commander','operations','planning','logistics','span of control','3-7','5']],['Application',['command post','staging','treatment area','document','briefing','consent']]],model:'CERT trains community volunteers to help themselves, their families and neighbors after a disaster, when professional responders are overwhelmed or delayed; CERT members support, but never replace, fire, EMS and police. Their first priority is rescuer safety: wearing PPE—helmet, eye protection, work gloves, mask and sturdy shoes—working in buddy teams and completing a size-up before acting. Their goal is to do the greatest good for the greatest number of people with the time and resources available, which is why they triage before giving detailed care and avoid dangerous rescues such as entering heavily damaged buildings. After an earthquake, the team organizes using the Incident Command System. An incident commander or team leader sets objectives and establishes a command post. The operations section sends teams for fire suppression, search and rescue and medical care; the planning section documents what has been found and done; and the logistics section gathers supplies, equipment and volunteers. Leaders keep a span of control of about 3 to 7 people, ideally 5. The team sets up a staging area and treatment areas, documents victims and hazards, obtains consent from conscious patients (unconscious patients give implied consent) and briefs professional responders when they arrive.'},sources:[RULES,IS317,READY_CERT]};

export const K2={title:'Disaster preparedness',hook:'The best time to prepare for a disaster is before it happens. Families with a plan, a kit and practiced skills cope better, recover faster and can help others instead of needing help. Disaster preparedness is 15% of the CERT test and the foundation of everything CERT members do.',objectives:['Identify natural, technological and intentional hazards and how to respond to each.','Build a disaster supply kit and a family emergency plan.','Explain shelter-in-place, evacuation and protective actions for common disasters.','Reduce hazards in homes, schools and workplaces.'],sections:[
['Types of hazards',[
'**Natural hazards** include earthquakes, floods, hurricanes, tornadoes, wildfires, winter storms, extreme heat, landslides and tsunamis, plus pandemics. **Technological hazards** include hazardous materials spills, power outages, dam failures and nuclear power plant emergencies. **Intentional hazards** include terrorism and civil unrest. Communities assess which hazards are most likely and plan accordingly.',
'The **emergency management cycle** has four phases: **mitigation** (reducing risk, such as building codes or securing furniture), **preparedness** (plans, kits, training), **response** (actions during and right after the disaster) and **recovery** (rebuilding and returning to normal). CERT members work in all four.',
order('Emergency management phases',['Mitigation','Preparedness','Response','Recovery']),
sort('Hazard type',['Natural','Technological','Intentional'],[['Hurricane',0],['Chemical plant leak',1],['Bombing',2],['Wildfire',0],['Widespread power grid failure',1],['Cyberattack on utilities',2]])]],
['Building a kit',[
'Ready.gov recommends supplies to last **at least several days**. A basic kit includes **water—one gallon per person per day**, non-perishable **food**, a battery-powered or hand-crank **radio** (NOAA weather radio), **flashlight**, **first aid kit**, extra **batteries**, a **whistle** to signal for help, **dust masks**, plastic sheeting and duct tape, moist towelettes and garbage bags for sanitation, a **wrench or pliers** to turn off utilities, a manual can opener, local maps and a cell phone with chargers and a backup battery.',
'Add **prescription medications**, glasses, infant formula and diapers, pet food, copies of important documents in a waterproof container, cash, sleeping bags, a change of clothing, a fire extinguisher, matches in a waterproof container and personal hygiene items. Keep a **home kit**, a smaller **work kit** and a **car kit**, and refresh food, water and batteries regularly.',
sort('Basic kit item?',['Include in the basic kit','Not an essential item'],[['One gallon of water per person per day',0],['Battery-powered or hand-crank radio',0],['Whistle',0],['Wrench to turn off utilities',0],['Fragile glass decorations',1],['Perishable fresh fruit only',1]])]],
['Making a plan',[
'A **family emergency plan** answers: How will we receive alerts? Where will we shelter? What is our evacuation route? How will we communicate? Choose **two meeting places**—one just outside the home (for fires) and one outside the neighborhood—and an **out-of-area contact** person, because long-distance calls or texts often get through when local lines are jammed. **Text messages** often work when calls do not.',
'Consider the needs of everyone: **older adults**, **people with disabilities**, **children**, people who need **medical equipment** or medications and **pets**. Practice the plan with drills twice a year, including fire escape routes with two ways out of every room. Sign up for local **emergency alerts** and know the **Wireless Emergency Alerts** and **Emergency Alert System**.',
flip('Plan essentials',[['Meeting place 1','Just outside the home'],['Meeting place 2','Outside the neighborhood'],['Out-of-area contact','Relays messages for the family'],['Text first','Texts often get through when calls fail'],['Special needs','Medications, equipment, pets, older adults'],['Practice','Drills at least twice a year']])]],
['Protective actions',[
'**Earthquakes:** **Drop, Cover and Hold On**—drop to hands and knees, cover your head and neck under a sturdy table or against an interior wall, and hold on until shaking stops; do not run outside or stand in doorways. **Tornadoes:** go to a basement or an interior room on the lowest floor away from windows. **Floods:** “**Turn Around, Don’t Drown**”—never walk or drive through flood water; just 6 inches of moving water can knock a person down. **Hurricanes:** follow evacuation orders.',
'**Shelter-in-place** means staying inside, often in an interior room, during chemical releases or severe weather; for chemical hazards, close windows, turn off ventilation and seal openings with plastic sheeting and tape. **Evacuation** means leaving the area by recommended routes—take your kit, tell your out-of-area contact and never ignore an order to leave. **Extreme heat** calls for cool places, water and checking on vulnerable neighbors.',
match('Disaster and protective action',[['Earthquake','Drop, Cover and Hold On'],['Tornado','Interior room on the lowest floor'],['Flood','Turn Around, Don’t Drown'],['Chemical release','Shelter in place and seal the room'],['Hurricane evacuation order','Leave by recommended routes'],['Extreme heat','Cool places, water and check on neighbors']])]],
['Hazard mitigation at home',[
'Mitigation reduces damage before a disaster. Perform a **home hazard hunt**: secure tall furniture, bookcases and water heaters to wall studs; move heavy objects to low shelves; install latches on cabinets; know how to shut off gas, water and electricity; install and test **smoke alarms** monthly and **carbon monoxide** alarms; keep fire extinguishers accessible; clear brush around homes in wildfire areas; and store flammable liquids safely away from heat sources.',
'Communities mitigate with building codes, flood maps, warning systems and land-use planning. Insurance (including flood insurance, which standard homeowners policies usually do not cover) speeds recovery.',
"Pandemics are also disasters. Preparedness includes vaccination, hand hygiene, staying home when sick, keeping a supply of medications and planning for school or work closures. Public health officials guide protective actions such as masking, testing and isolation.",
sort('Mitigation or not?',['Reduces disaster damage','Increases risk'],[['Strapping the water heater to wall studs',0],['Heavy items on high shelves',1],['Testing smoke alarms monthly',0],['Storing gasoline near the furnace',1],['Clearing brush near a house in a wildfire zone',0],['Blocking exits with furniture',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Earthquake response',{a:{text:'You are in a classroom when an earthquake starts. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Drop, Cover under a desk and Hold On','b','Correct. Protect your head and neck.',true],['Run outside during the shaking','b','Falling debris near exits is dangerous.',false]]},b:{text:'After the shaking stops, you smell gas in the hallway. What next?',choices:[['Leave the building, avoid sparks and report the gas leak','c','Correct. Do not use switches or open flames.',true],['Turn on the lights to look for the leak','c','Electrical switches can ignite gas.',false]]},c:{text:'You meet at the designated area, account for classmates and follow the school plan.',end:true}})]],
["Community preparedness and CERT activities",["Preparedness extends beyond the home. **Schools and workplaces** have emergency plans, drills, evacuation routes and supply caches; students should know their school’s procedures for lockdowns, shelter-in-place and evacuation. **Neighborhoods** can map residents who may need help (older adults, people with disabilities), share skills and identify tools such as generators and chain saws. **Community warning systems** include sirens, Wireless Emergency Alerts and local media.","CERT members support preparedness by teaching classes, staffing information booths, helping with drills and joining organizations such as the **Medical Reserve Corps** or local volunteer groups. Recovery begins quickly after a disaster: documenting damage with photos for insurance, avoiding scams, getting accurate information from official sources and taking care of mental health.",{"t":"check","q":"Why should neighborhoods identify residents who may need extra help?","options":["So volunteers can check on them quickly after a disaster","To share private information online","To avoid them","It is not useful"],"why":"Older adults and people with disabilities may need assistance."}]]
],takeaways:['Hazards are natural, technological or intentional; emergency management phases are mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery.','Kits hold at least several days of supplies, including 1 gallon of water per person per day, food, radio, flashlight, first aid kit, whistle, masks and a wrench; plans include two meeting places and an out-of-area contact.','Drop, Cover and Hold On; interior rooms for tornadoes; Turn Around, Don’t Drown; shelter in place or evacuate as directed; mitigate home hazards and test alarms.'],terms:[['Mitigation','Actions that reduce disaster risk before it happens.'],['Shelter in place','Staying inside a protected room during a hazard.'],['Out-of-area contact','A distant person who relays family messages.']],mcqs:[q('How much water should a kit contain per person per day?','One gallon','One cup','One liter','Five gallons','For drinking and sanitation.'),q('What is the correct earthquake action?','Drop, Cover and Hold On','Run outside','Stand in a doorway','Get in an elevator','Protect your head and neck.'),q('Where should you go during a tornado?','An interior room on the lowest floor','Upstairs near windows','Outside in a car','Under a bridge','Away from windows.'),q('Which phase includes strapping a water heater to the wall?','Mitigation','Response','Recovery','Rescue','Reduces damage.'),q('Why designate an out-of-area contact?','Distant calls and texts often get through when local lines are jammed','To call 911','To store supplies','It is not useful','Relays messages.'),q('How much moving water can knock a person down?','About 6 inches','About 6 feet','Only deep rivers','Water cannot','Turn Around, Don’t Drown.'),q('Which hazard is technological?','Chemical spill','Hurricane','Earthquake','Wildfire','Human-made.'),q('How often should smoke alarms be tested?','Monthly','Once every 10 years','Never','Only after a fire','Replace batteries as needed.')],frq:{prompt:'Create a disaster preparedness plan for a family of four with a grandparent who uses a wheelchair and a dog, living in an earthquake- and flood-prone area.',rubric:[['Kit',['water','gallon','food','radio','flashlight','first aid','medications','whistle','pet']],['Plan',['meeting place','out-of-area contact','text','alerts','evacuation','route']],['Special needs',['wheelchair','accessible','medications','grandparent','pet','carrier']],['Protective actions and mitigation',['drop','cover','hold on','turn around','flood','secure','furniture','smoke alarms','practice']]],model:'The family builds a home kit with at least several days of supplies: one gallon of water per person per day plus water for the dog, non-perishable food, a hand-crank radio, flashlights, extra batteries, a first aid kit, a whistle, dust masks, sanitation supplies, a wrench to shut off utilities, the grandparent’s medications and medical information, copies of documents and cash, plus dog food, a leash, a carrier and vaccination records. Their plan includes alerts on every phone, a meeting place outside the home and another outside the neighborhood, an out-of-area relative as a contact and texting instead of calling when networks are busy. Because the grandparent uses a wheelchair, the family identifies accessible evacuation routes and shelters, keeps spare wheelchair batteries or a manual chair, arranges help from neighbors and keeps a list of medications and doctors. For earthquakes, they practice Drop, Cover and Hold On and, to reduce damage, secure bookcases, the water heater and heavy items. For floods, they learn their evacuation route, never walk or drive through flood water—Turn Around, Don’t Drown—and keep important items high. They test smoke and carbon monoxide alarms monthly, refresh supplies regularly and practice the plan twice a year.'},sources:[RULES,READY_KIT,READY_PLAN,READY_QUAKE,IS317]};

export const K3={title:'Fire safety, utility control and hazardous materials',hook:'After an earthquake, broken gas lines and electrical damage can start fires faster than firefighters can respond. CERT members learn to put out small fires safely, shut off utilities and recognize hazardous materials—and, just as important, when to walk away. Fire safety is 5% of the test, but its lessons protect rescuers in every skill.',objectives:['Explain the fire triangle and classes of fire.','Select and use a portable fire extinguisher with the PASS technique and safe size-up.','Shut off gas, electricity and water safely after a disaster.','Recognize hazardous materials using placards and the NFPA 704 diamond.'],sections:[
['How fire works',[
'Fire needs **heat**, **fuel** and **oxygen**—the **fire triangle**; a fourth element, the **chemical chain reaction**, makes the **fire tetrahedron**. Removing any element puts the fire out: water removes heat, a lid or foam removes oxygen and shutting off gas removes fuel. Fires grow quickly—a room can reach **flashover**, where everything ignites at once, within minutes.',
'Fires are classified by fuel: **Class A**—ordinary combustibles (wood, paper, cloth); **Class B**—flammable liquids and gases (gasoline, oil, propane); **Class C**—energized electrical equipment; **Class D**—combustible metals (magnesium); **Class K**—cooking oils and fats in commercial kitchens. Using water on a grease or electrical fire can spread fire or cause electrocution.',
match('Fire class and fuel',[['Class A','Wood, paper and cloth'],['Class B','Flammable liquids and gases'],['Class C','Energized electrical equipment'],['Class D','Combustible metals'],['Class K','Cooking oils and fats'],['Fire triangle','Heat, fuel and oxygen']])]],
['Extinguishers and the PASS technique',[
'Portable extinguishers are labeled with the fire classes they fight. **ABC multipurpose dry chemical** extinguishers are common in homes; **water** extinguishers work only on Class A; **carbon dioxide** works on B and C. Use the **PASS** technique: **P**ull the pin, **A**im low at the base of the fire, **S**queeze the handle and **S**weep side to side. Most extinguishers last only about **10 seconds**, so stand about **6–10 feet** away and approach as the fire shrinks.',
'CERT members fight only **small fires** they can control. Before approaching, **size up**: Is the fire small and contained? Is the right extinguisher available? Is there a **clear exit behind you**? Is the air safe to breathe? Work with a **buddy** (one fights, one backs up), keep the **exit at your back** and never let the fire get between you and the exit. If the fire grows, leave, close the door and call the fire department. **Overhaul**—checking for hidden embers—is left to professionals.',
order('PASS',['Pull the pin','Aim at the base of the fire','Squeeze the handle','Sweep side to side']),
sort('Fight it or leave?',['Small fire—may fight with the right extinguisher','Leave and call the fire department'],[['Wastebasket fire with a clear exit',0],['Fire spreading up walls to the ceiling',1],['Room filling with smoke',1],['Small stovetop pan fire with a lid available',0],['Fire between you and the exit',1],['No extinguisher available',1]])]],
['Utility control',[
'**Natural gas:** shut off gas **only if** you **smell gas, hear a hissing sound or see damaged lines**. Use a wrench to turn the valve at the meter a **quarter turn** so it is **crosswise** to the pipe. Once off, **only the gas company should turn it back on**. Do not use matches, lighters, electrical switches or phones near a gas leak.',
'**Electricity:** turn off the main breaker or pull the main fuse if there is damage, flooding or sparking—turn off individual circuits first, then the main. Never touch power lines or stand in water near electrical equipment. **Water:** shut off the main valve if pipes are broken to prevent flooding and to protect clean water in the water heater, which can serve as emergency drinking water.',
order('Gas leak response',['Recognize gas odor, hissing or damage','Avoid sparks, flames and switches','Get people out','Turn the meter valve a quarter turn crosswise to the pipe','Leave it off; only the utility restores it','Report to the gas company or fire department'])]],
['Hazardous materials',[
'**Hazardous materials (hazmat)** may be present in homes, businesses and transportation. Clues include **placards** on vehicles and buildings, **labels**, unusual odors, clouds or spills, and people or animals showing symptoms. The **NFPA 704 diamond** on buildings uses colors and numbers 0–4: **blue = health**, **red = flammability**, **yellow = instability/reactivity** and **white = special hazards** (such as **W** with a line through it for water-reactive or **OX** for oxidizers). Higher numbers mean greater danger.',
'Transport vehicles carry **DOT placards** with a **UN/NA number** that can be looked up in the **Emergency Response Guidebook (ERG)**. CERT members **do not** handle hazmat incidents: stay **upwind, uphill and upstream**, isolate the area, keep people away and report to authorities.',
match('NFPA 704 colors',[['Blue','Health hazard'],['Red','Flammability'],['Yellow','Instability or reactivity'],['White','Special hazards'],['4','Most severe rating'],['Upwind, uphill, upstream','Where to stay during a hazmat release']])]],
['Fire prevention',[
'Most home fires start in the **kitchen**. Never leave cooking unattended, keep flammable items away from stoves and heaters, use space heaters with at least **3 feet** of clearance, avoid overloading outlets, store flammable liquids safely, use flashlights instead of candles after disasters and install smoke alarms on every level and outside sleeping areas. Practice home fire escape plans with two exits from each room.',
"If clothing catches fire, **Stop, Drop and Roll**—stop where you are, drop to the ground, cover your face and roll until the flames are out. In smoke, **crawl low** where the air is cleaner, and once out, **stay out**.",
kase('Fire decision',{a:{text:'After an earthquake, you and your partner find a small fire in a trash can in an office. The exit is behind you, and you have an ABC extinguisher. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Size up, keep the exit at your back and use PASS while your buddy backs you up','b','Correct. Small fire, right tool, safe exit.',true],['Use your hands to smother it','b','Use the extinguisher, not your hands.',false]]},b:{text:'The fire flares up and spreads to the wall. What now?',choices:[['Leave, close the door and report to the fire department','c','Correct. Do not fight a growing fire.',true],['Keep spraying until the extinguisher is empty','c','A spreading fire is beyond CERT capability.',false]]},c:{text:'You account for your partner, report the fire’s location and mark the building.',end:true}})]],
["Fire suppression teamwork and protective equipment",["CERT fire suppression uses a **buddy system**: one person operates the extinguisher while the backup watches the fire, the exit and the extinguisher’s remaining charge, ready to take over or call a retreat. Teams approach from the **upwind** side when outdoors, stay low to avoid heat and smoke, and **never enter smoke-filled rooms**. After extinguishing a small fire, they back away while watching for re-ignition and report it so firefighters can perform overhaul.","PPE protects from heat, debris and smoke, but CERT gear is **not** firefighter turnout gear. Dust masks do not protect against smoke or toxic gases. If a door feels **hot** to the back of the hand, do not open it—fire may be on the other side. Know the location of exits and fire alarms in every building, and practice extinguisher use under supervision before a real emergency.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Safe fire suppression practice?","buckets":["Safe","Unsafe"],"items":[["Working with a backup buddy",0],["Entering a smoke-filled room",1],["Feeling a door with the back of your hand",0],["Turning your back on the fire after it seems out",1],["Approaching an outdoor fire from upwind",0],["Relying on a dust mask to protect against smoke",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen (plus a chain reaction); classes A, B, C, D and K; never use water on grease or electrical fires.','PASS: Pull, Aim at the base, Squeeze, Sweep; fight only small fires with a buddy, the right extinguisher and the exit at your back; leave if the fire grows.','Shut off gas only for odor, hissing or damage (quarter turn crosswise; utility restores); turn off electricity and water as needed; recognize hazmat with placards and NFPA 704 and stay upwind, uphill and upstream.'],terms:[['Fire triangle','Heat, fuel and oxygen needed for fire.'],['PASS','Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep.'],['NFPA 704','A color-coded diamond showing hazards of stored materials.']],mcqs:[q('Which elements make up the fire triangle?','Heat, fuel and oxygen','Water, wind and earth','Smoke, flame and ash','Carbon, nitrogen and hydrogen','Remove one to extinguish.'),q('A grease fire in a commercial kitchen is Class:','K','A','C','D','Cooking oils.'),q('What does the first S in PASS stand for?','Squeeze','Spray','Stop','Smother','Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep.'),q('Where should you aim the extinguisher?','At the base of the fire','At the flames’ tips','At the ceiling','At the smoke','Fuel source.'),q('When should a CERT member shut off natural gas?','When gas is smelled or heard or lines are damaged','After every storm','Never','Every night','Only the utility restores it.'),q('In the NFPA 704 diamond, red indicates:','Flammability','Health','Reactivity','Special hazards','Blue health, yellow reactivity.'),q('During a hazmat release, stay:','Upwind, uphill and upstream','Downwind','Close to inspect','Inside the cloud','Avoid exposure.'),q('A gas meter valve is off when it is:','Crosswise to the pipe','Parallel to the pipe','Removed','Painted','Quarter turn.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a CERT team decides whether to fight a fire and how to use an extinguisher safely, and describe how to control natural gas after an earthquake.',rubric:[['Fire size-up',['small','contained','extinguisher','class','exit','behind','buddy','smoke']],['PASS',['pull','aim','base','squeeze','sweep','6-10 feet','10 seconds']],['When to leave',['grows','spreads','close the door','fire department','between']],['Gas control',['smell','hiss','damage','wrench','quarter turn','crosswise','no sparks','utility company']]],model:'Before fighting a fire, the team sizes it up: the fire must be small and contained, the right extinguisher must be available for the fire class (for example, ABC for ordinary combustibles, liquids or electrical fires), there must be a clear exit behind the rescuers and the air must be safe to breathe. One member fights while the buddy backs them up, both wearing PPE, with the exit at their backs. Standing about 6–10 feet away, the rescuer uses PASS: pull the pin, aim low at the base of the fire, squeeze the handle and sweep side to side, moving closer as the fire shrinks, remembering that most extinguishers last only about 10 seconds. If the fire grows, spreads up walls, fills the room with smoke or gets between them and the exit, they leave immediately, close the door to slow it and report it to the fire department. After an earthquake, they shut off natural gas only if they smell gas, hear hissing or see damaged lines. They avoid sparks, flames, electrical switches and phones near the leak, get people out and use a wrench to turn the meter valve a quarter turn so it is crosswise to the pipe. They leave it off, because only the gas company should turn it back on, and report the leak.'},sources:[RULES,USFA_EXT,PHMSA_ERG,IS317]};

export const K4={title:'Treating life-threatening conditions I: the airway and recovery position (Skill II-A)',hook:'In a disaster, the three conditions that kill people fastest are an obstructed airway, uncontrolled bleeding and shock. A person whose airway is blocked can die within minutes, yet the fix—correct head and jaw positioning—takes seconds. Skill II-A tests opening the airway with the jaw-thrust maneuver and placing a patient in the recovery position.',objectives:['Recognize the “three killers” and why airway comes first.','Check responsiveness and breathing safely.','Open the airway with the jaw-thrust maneuver following HOSA Skill II-A.','Place a breathing patient in the recovery position.'],sections:[
['The three killers',[
'CERT medical operations focus on the **“three killers”**: **airway obstruction**, **excessive bleeding** and **shock**. They are treated rapidly—often during triage—because they cause death within minutes. More detailed care, such as splinting and bandaging minor wounds, comes later in treatment areas.',
'In an unconscious person, the most common airway obstruction is the **tongue** falling back against the throat. Other causes include vomit, blood, teeth and debris. Opening the airway lets air move again and is the first lifesaving step for a person who is not breathing.',
order('Order of priority',['Rescuer safety and PPE','Airway','Bleeding','Shock','Other injuries'])]],
['Approaching and assessing: Skill II-A steps 1–5',[
'Skill II-A is performed by **Rescuer 1** in **5 minutes**, beginning on a manikin. **1.** Don **PPE**—helmet, eye protection, work gloves, mask and sturdy shoes (helmet, boots and gloves may be verbalized). **2.** **Size up** the scene. **3.** Identify an **unconscious patient**. **4.** At **arm’s distance**, **touch the shoulder and shout**, “**Can you hear me?**” (judge: “No response”). **5.** **Check for breathing** (judge: “No breathing”).',
'Checking at arm’s distance protects the rescuer from sudden movement. Breathing is checked by looking for chest rise and listening and feeling for air. Because disaster patients may have head and neck injuries, CERT teaches the **jaw-thrust** maneuver, which opens the airway without tilting the head.',
order('Steps before opening the airway',['Don PPE','Size up the scene','Identify an unconscious patient','At arm’s distance, touch the shoulder and shout “Can you hear me?”','Check for breathing'])]],
['The jaw-thrust maneuver: Skill II-A step 6',[
'**6a.** **Kneel at the patient’s head.** **6b.** Place **one hand on each side of the patient’s head**, with your **thumbs near the corners of the mouth pointed toward the chin**, using your **elbows for support**. **6c.** **Slide your fingers into position under the angles of the patient’s jawbone**—without moving the head or neck—and lift the jaw forward. This pulls the tongue away from the back of the throat.',
'The judge then says, “Attempt to open airway successful, and patient begins to breathe on their own.” If a patient does not begin breathing after one attempt to open the airway (or, for a child, after two rescue breaths in SALT), the patient is considered **dead** in mass-casualty triage and the rescuer moves on—because time spent on one patient who cannot survive could cost other lives.',
order('Jaw thrust',['Kneel at the head','Hands on each side of the head','Thumbs near the corners of the mouth, pointing to the chin','Elbows for support','Fingers under the angles of the jaw','Lift the jaw forward without moving the head or neck']),
chk('Why does CERT teach the jaw thrust rather than tilting the head?',['It opens the airway without moving a possibly injured neck','It is faster to explain','It works only on children','It closes the airway'],'Disaster victims may have neck injuries.')]],
['The recovery position: Skill II-A step 7',[
'When the patient begins breathing (a live patient may replace the manikin here), place them in the **recovery position** so fluids drain and the airway stays open: **a.** lay the body on its **side**; **b.** extend the **bottom arm outward**; **c.** place the **top arm on the bicep of the bottom arm**; **d.** rest the **head on the hand**; **e.** **bend the top leg (or both legs)** to keep the body stable; **f.** **raise the chin forward**; and **g.** point the **mouth downward**. **8.** Communicate with the patient and partner throughout.',
'Continue to monitor breathing. If the patient vomits, the downward-pointing mouth lets it drain. In a disaster, CERT members may need to leave a stable patient to help others and return to check on them.',
order('Recovery position',['Roll onto the side','Bottom arm out','Top arm on the bottom arm’s bicep','Head on the hand','Bend the top or both legs','Chin forward','Mouth pointed down']),
flip('Skill II-A key points',[['Responsiveness check','Touch the shoulder and shout at arm’s distance'],['Airway maneuver','Jaw thrust without moving the head or neck'],['Thumb position','Near the corners of the mouth toward the chin'],['Finger position','Under the angles of the jaw'],['Recovery position','Side-lying, chin forward, mouth down'],['Rescuer','Rescuer 1 performs II-A']])]],
['Other airway problems',[
'For a **conscious choking** adult who cannot speak, cough or breathe, give **abdominal thrusts** (and back blows as trained) until the object comes out or the person becomes unresponsive. People with breathing difficulty from asthma or allergic reactions should be helped to use their own inhaler or epinephrine auto-injector. **Smoke inhalation**, **dust** and **toxic fumes** can damage airways—move patients to fresh air if safe and wear a mask yourself.',
'Children have smaller airways and tongues that more easily block them. In SALT triage, a child who is not breathing after the airway is opened receives **two rescue breaths** before being categorized as dead. CPR is generally not performed during the triage phase of a mass-casualty incident because it ties up rescuers needed to save many others.',
"In everyday emergencies with enough rescuers, CPR and an AED are started for anyone unresponsive and not breathing normally; in a mass-casualty disaster with few rescuers, triage priorities come first. CERT members should take a separate CPR/AED course to be ready for both situations.",
kase('Airway first',{a:{text:'You find an unresponsive man lying face up under debris after an earthquake. (Scenario invented for practice.) After touching his shoulder and shouting, he does not respond and is not breathing. What next?',choices:[['Kneel at his head and open the airway with a jaw thrust','b','Correct. Protect the neck while opening the airway.',true],['Tilt his head far back quickly','b','A head tilt could worsen a neck injury.',false]]},b:{text:'He starts breathing. What do you do?',choices:[['Place him in the recovery position and monitor','c','Correct. Keeps the airway open and lets fluids drain.',true],['Leave him on his back','c','The tongue or vomit may block the airway again.',false]]},c:{text:'You tag him, tell your partner and continue triage, returning to recheck him.',end:true}})]],
["Choking, breathing problems and when to move a patient",["For a conscious **choking** adult who cannot speak, cough or breathe, ask “Are you choking? May I help?” and give **abdominal thrusts**—stand behind, place a fist just above the navel, grasp it with the other hand and thrust inward and upward—until the object comes out or the person becomes unresponsive. For pregnant or very large patients, use **chest thrusts**. If the person becomes unresponsive, lower them to the ground, call for help and check the airway.","Move a patient before treating only when the scene is **unsafe** (fire, gas, collapse risk) or when they must be moved to reach others who need care. Otherwise, treat in place and avoid moving anyone with possible head, neck or back injuries. Patients in respiratory distress are often most comfortable **sitting up**; do not force them to lie down.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Abdominal thrusts for a conscious choking adult","steps":["Ask “Are you choking? May I help?”","Stand behind the person","Place a fist just above the navel","Grasp the fist with your other hand","Thrust inward and upward","Repeat until the object comes out or the person becomes unresponsive"]}]]
],takeaways:['The three killers are airway obstruction, excessive bleeding and shock; the tongue is the most common airway obstruction in unconscious people.','Skill II-A: PPE, size-up, identify the unconscious patient, touch the shoulder and shout at arm’s distance, check breathing, then jaw thrust—kneel at the head, thumbs near the mouth corners, fingers under the jaw angles, no head or neck movement.','Recovery position: side-lying, bottom arm out, top arm on the bottom bicep, head on the hand, legs bent, chin forward, mouth down; in triage, not breathing after one airway attempt (two breaths for a child) is dead.'],terms:[['Jaw-thrust maneuver','Opening the airway by lifting the jaw without moving the head or neck.'],['Recovery position','A side-lying position that keeps the airway open.'],['Three killers','Airway obstruction, excessive bleeding and shock.']],mcqs:[q('What are CERT’s “three killers”?','Airway obstruction, excessive bleeding and shock','Fire, flood and wind','Burns, fractures and sprains','Heat, cold and thirst','Treated first.'),q('The most common airway obstruction in an unconscious person is:','The tongue','A coin','Teeth','A broken bone','Falls back against the throat.'),q('How do you check responsiveness?','At arm’s distance, touch the shoulder and shout','Pour water on the face','Pinch hard','Lift the patient','Per Skill II-A.'),q('In the jaw thrust, where are the thumbs placed?','Near the corners of the mouth pointing toward the chin','On the forehead','On the chest','On the ears','Fingers go under the jaw angles.'),q('Why is the jaw thrust used?','It opens the airway without moving the head or neck','It is less effective','It closes the mouth','It is used only on infants','Possible spine injury.'),q('In the recovery position, the mouth points:','Downward','Upward','Toward the sky','Into the pillow','Allows drainage.'),q('Which rescuer performs Skill II-A?','Rescuer 1','Rescuer 2','The judge','Both together','Rescuer 2 controls bleeding.'),q('In SALT, a child who is not breathing after the airway is opened receives:','Two rescue breaths','Ten minutes of CPR','Nothing','A tourniquet','Then is categorized.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how Rescuer 1 completes HOSA Skill II-A, from donning PPE to placing the patient in the recovery position, and explain why each key step is done.',rubric:[['Safety and assessment',['ppe','helmet','size-up','unconscious','arm’s distance','shoulder','shout','breathing']],['Jaw thrust',['kneel','head','thumbs','corners of the mouth','chin','elbows','angles of the jaw','neck']],['Recovery position',['side','bottom arm','bicep','head on the hand','bend','chin','mouth downward']],['Reasoning',['tongue','spine','drain','monitor','communicate','partner']]],model:'I put on my PPE—helmet, eye protection, work gloves, mask and sturdy shoes—so I do not become a victim, and I size up the scene for hazards. I identify an unconscious patient and, from arm’s distance so I am not struck if they move suddenly, touch the shoulder and shout, “Can you hear me?” There is no response, and when I check for breathing, there is none. Because disaster victims may have neck injuries, I open the airway with a jaw thrust instead of tilting the head: I kneel at the patient’s head, place one hand on each side of the head with my thumbs near the corners of the mouth pointed toward the chin and my elbows supported, slide my fingers under the angles of the jawbone and lift the jaw forward without moving the head or neck. This pulls the tongue, the most common cause of obstruction, away from the back of the throat. When the patient begins to breathe, I place them in the recovery position: on their side with the bottom arm reaching outward, the top arm resting on the bicep of the bottom arm, the head on the hand, the top or both legs bent for stability, the chin raised forward and the mouth pointed downward so fluids drain and the airway stays open. I keep talking to the patient and my partner and continue to monitor breathing.'},sources:[RULES,IS317,MEDLINE_RECOVERY,REDCROSS_FA]};

export const K5={title:'Treating life-threatening conditions II: bleeding control, tourniquets and shock (Skill II-B)',hook:'A person can bleed to death from a damaged artery in as little as three to five minutes—faster than an ambulance can arrive even on a normal day. Firm pressure and, when needed, a tourniquet save lives. Skill II-B tests Rescuer 2 on controlling bleeding, applying a tourniquet and assessing and treating shock.',objectives:['Recognize arterial, venous and capillary bleeding.','Control bleeding with direct pressure and a tourniquet following HOSA Skill II-B.','Assess for shock using breathing, capillary refill and mental status.','Maintain body temperature and complete glove removal and hand hygiene.'],sections:[
['Types of bleeding',[
'**Arterial bleeding** is bright red and **spurts** with each heartbeat—the most dangerous type. **Venous bleeding** is darker red and **flows** steadily. **Capillary bleeding** **oozes** slowly from scrapes. Life-threatening bleeding is blood that is spurting or flowing continuously, pooling on the ground, soaking through clothing or bandages, or causing confusion or loss of consciousness.',
'The main methods to control bleeding are **direct pressure** (most effective first step), **packing** deep wounds with gauze (especially junctional areas such as the groin or armpit) and **tourniquets** for life-threatening limb bleeding that pressure does not control. Elevation may help but should not delay pressure.',
match('Bleeding type',[['Arterial','Bright red and spurting'],['Venous','Dark red and flowing steadily'],['Capillary','Slow oozing from scrapes'],['Direct pressure','First step to control bleeding'],['Wound packing','Gauze pushed into deep wounds'],['Tourniquet','For life-threatening limb bleeding']])]],
['Approaching the patient: Skill II-B steps 1–8',[
'Skill II-B is performed by **Rescuer 2** in **5 minutes**, starting with a live patient. **1.** Don PPE. **2.** Size up. **3.** Identify the patient with uncontrolled bleeding. **4.** Make sure the patient **can see you approach**. **5.** **Identify yourself** by name and organization. **6.** **Request permission to treat**. **7.** **Identify the source** of bleeding (arm or leg). **8.** Apply **firm, steady pressure** with sterile **4 × 4 gauze directly on the source**, and verbalize **pushing hard even if the patient complains of pain**.',
'Approaching from the front, introducing yourself and asking permission calm the patient and respect their rights. The judge then says, “Wound is still bleeding and EMS has been delayed.” From this point a **manikin arm or pool noodle** replaces the live patient for the tourniquet—**never apply a tourniquet to a live volunteer**.',
order('Before the tourniquet',['PPE and size-up','Identify the bleeding patient','Approach so the patient can see you','State your name and organization','Ask permission to treat','Find the source of bleeding','Firm direct pressure with sterile gauze'])]],
['Applying a tourniquet: Skill II-B step 9',[
'**9a.** Obtain a **commercial tourniquet**. **9b.** Place it **at least 2–3 inches above the wound**, or **as high as possible on the injured limb** (closest to the torso)—it can go **over clothing**. **9c.** Make sure the **two ends are clipped together** with the buckle or clasp. **9d.** **Reassure the patient** that tightening may be painful but is necessary. **9e.** **Secure** the tourniquet using the method for that device (tighten the windlass until bleeding stops, then lock it). **9f.** **Note the time** it was applied. **9g.** The tourniquet **stays in place until EMS arrives**—CERT members do not loosen or remove it.',
'Writing the time on the tourniquet or the patient’s forehead tells medical staff how long blood flow has been stopped. A properly applied tourniquet hurts; that is expected. If bleeding continues, a second tourniquet may be placed above the first.',
"Signs that a tourniquet is working: the bleeding stops and there is no pulse below it. A loose tourniquet can increase bleeding by blocking veins but not arteries, so tighten until bleeding stops.",
order('Tourniquet application',['Obtain a commercial tourniquet','Place 2–3 inches above the wound or high on the limb','Clip the ends together','Warn the patient it will hurt','Tighten and secure until bleeding stops','Note the time','Leave in place until EMS arrives']),
chk('When should a CERT member remove a tourniquet?',['Never—leave it for EMS or medical staff','After 10 minutes','When the patient asks','When bleeding stops'],'Only medical professionals remove tourniquets.')]],
['Assessing shock: Skill II-B step 10',[
'**Shock** occurs when the body’s organs do not get enough blood and oxygen; untreated, it leads to death. Assess three signs: **10a.** **rapid, shallow breathing**; **10b.** **capillary refill greater than 2 seconds** (press the nail bed or skin and watch how quickly color returns); and **10c.** **inability to follow simple commands**—for example, ask the patient to **squeeze your hand**. The judge tells you the results.',
'Other signs include pale, cool, clammy skin, a rapid weak pulse, anxiety, confusion and thirst. If signs of shock are present, go to step 11; if not, skip to step 12. In triage, these same checks—breathing, perfusion and mental status—help decide who is **Immediate**.',
sort('Sign of shock?',['Possible sign of shock','Normal finding'],[['Rapid, shallow breathing',0],['Capillary refill of 4 seconds',0],['Cannot follow the command to squeeze your hand',0],['Capillary refill under 2 seconds',1],['Pale, cool, clammy skin',0],['Squeezes your hand on command',1]])]],
['Treating shock and finishing: Skill II-B steps 11–15',[
'**11.** Maintain body temperature: **a.** verbalize **removing wet clothing** (if the scenario says so); **b.** place something between the patient and the **ground** (cardboard, a jacket or a blanket); **c.** **wrap** the patient in **dry layers**; **d.** **shield** them from wind with your body or surrounding objects. Keep the patient lying down and calm, and do not give food or drink.',
'**12.** **Remove gloves properly** without touching the outside with bare hands. **13.** Dispose of gloves safely. **14.** Use **alcohol-based hand rub**. **15.** Communicate with the patient and partner throughout. In real disasters, CERT members change gloves between patients to avoid spreading infection.',
order('Shock care',['Remove wet clothing if indicated','Insulate the patient from the ground','Wrap in dry layers','Shield from wind','Keep lying down and calm','Monitor and reassess'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Severe leg bleeding',{a:{text:'A woman has bright red blood spurting from her thigh. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is your first action after PPE, approach and permission?',choices:[['Apply firm direct pressure with sterile gauze on the source','b','Correct. Pressure first.',true],['Clean the wound','b','Stop the bleeding first.',false]]},b:{text:'Bleeding continues and EMS is delayed. Where do you place the tourniquet?',choices:[['At least 2–3 inches above the wound or high on the thigh, then tighten and note the time','c','Correct. Then leave it for EMS.',true],['Directly over the wound','c','Place it above the wound.',false]]},c:{text:'You assess shock: rapid breathing, refill over 2 seconds. You insulate her from the ground, wrap her in dry layers and shield her from wind.',end:true}})]],
["Wound packing and improvised tourniquets",["For deep wounds in places where a tourniquet cannot go—the **neck, armpit or groin**—**pack the wound**: push gauze (or the cleanest cloth available) firmly into the wound until it is full, then hold strong pressure for several minutes and add a pressure bandage. Pressure from the packing presses on the bleeding vessel deep inside the wound.","**Commercial tourniquets** are preferred because they work reliably. In a disaster without one, trained responders may improvise with a wide band (at least about 1.5–2 inches) and a stick as a windlass, but narrow cords or wire can cause injury and are not recommended. Always check that bleeding has stopped after tightening, note the time and never cover the tourniquet, so EMS can see it.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Bleeding-control choice","pairs":[["Spurting bleeding from the thigh","Pressure, then tourniquet if uncontrolled"],["Deep wound in the groin","Pack the wound and hold pressure"],["Scraped knee oozing slowly","Clean and cover"],["Tourniquet applied","Note the time; leave visible"],["Bleeding through the first dressing","Add more gauze and press harder"],["No commercial tourniquet","Wide improvised band with a windlass, if trained"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Arterial bleeding spurts bright red, venous flows dark red and capillary oozes; direct pressure first, pack deep wounds, tourniquet for life-threatening limb bleeding.','Skill II-B: PPE, size-up, let the patient see you, identify yourself, ask permission, find the source, firm pressure; tourniquet 2–3 inches above the wound or high on the limb, ends clipped, warn of pain, secure, note the time, leave for EMS—manikin only.','Shock signs: rapid shallow breathing, capillary refill over 2 seconds, cannot follow simple commands; keep warm (remove wet clothing, insulate from ground, dry layers, shield from wind); remove gloves properly and use hand rub.'],terms:[['Tourniquet','A band tightened above a limb wound to stop severe bleeding.'],['Shock','A life-threatening lack of blood flow to organs.'],['Capillary refill','Time for color to return after pressing on skin or a nail bed.']],mcqs:[q('Which bleeding is bright red and spurts?','Arterial','Venous','Capillary','Internal only','Most dangerous.'),q('What is the first step to control bleeding?','Direct pressure','Tourniquet','Ice','Elevation only','Push hard.'),q('Where is a tourniquet placed?','At least 2–3 inches above the wound or high on the limb','Directly on the wound','Below the wound','On the neck','Can go over clothing.'),q('After applying a tourniquet, you must:','Note the time','Loosen it every 5 minutes','Remove it when bleeding stops','Cover it so no one sees it','Leave it for EMS.'),q('On whom is the tourniquet demonstrated in Skill II-B?','A manikin arm or pool noodle','A live volunteer','The judge','Your partner','Never on a live patient.'),q('Which finding suggests shock?','Capillary refill greater than 2 seconds','Squeezing your hand on command','Normal slow breathing','Warm pink skin','Poor perfusion.'),q('How do you test mental status in Skill II-B?','Ask the patient to squeeze your hand','Shine a light in the eyes','Ask for their address only','Pinch the ear','Simple command.'),q('Which action helps maintain body temperature?','Place something between the patient and the ground','Remove all dry clothing','Pour cold water on the patient','Fan the patient','Insulate and wrap.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how Rescuer 2 controls severe bleeding from an arm and treats shock following HOSA Skill II-B.',rubric:[['Approach and consent',['ppe','size-up','see','approach','name','organization','permission']],['Direct pressure',['source','sterile','4 x 4','gauze','firm','push hard','pain']],['Tourniquet',['commercial','2-3 inches','high','clothing','clip','reassure','secure','time','ems']],['Shock assessment and care',['rapid','shallow','capillary refill','2 seconds','squeeze','wet clothing','ground','dry layers','wind','gloves']]],model:'After putting on PPE and sizing up the scene, I identify the patient with uncontrolled bleeding from the arm, approach so they can see me, tell them my name and organization and ask permission to treat. I find the source of bleeding and apply firm, steady pressure directly on it with sterile 4 × 4 gauze, pushing hard even if it hurts. When the wound keeps bleeding and EMS is delayed, I switch to the manikin arm and apply a commercial tourniquet at least 2–3 inches above the wound—or as high on the limb as possible—over clothing if needed. I make sure the ends are clipped together, warn the patient that tightening will hurt but is necessary, tighten and secure it until the bleeding stops and note the time it was applied. The tourniquet stays in place until EMS arrives. Then I assess for shock: I check for rapid, shallow breathing, press the nail bed to see whether capillary refill takes more than 2 seconds and ask the patient to squeeze my hand to test whether they can follow simple commands. If signs of shock are present, I maintain body temperature by removing wet clothing if indicated, placing cardboard, a jacket or a blanket between the patient and the ground, wrapping them in dry layers and shielding them from wind. Finally, I remove my gloves without touching the outsides, dispose of them safely, use alcohol-based hand rub and keep communicating with the patient and my partner.'},sources:[RULES,STOP_BLEED,MEDLINE_SHOCK,IS317]};
